  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1234 PM EST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nA large upper level ridge continues to amplify into the Midwest and \nGreat Lakes regions with the dry and very mild weather pattern \nexpected to last through this week. Surface high pressure will \nprevail with little movement from where it is anchored over the\nAppalachian mountains. Southerly flow and increased WAA will \nfunnel in much warmer air from the Gulf and southern US. There \nare no hazardous weather concerns this week as both high and low\ntemperatures will continue to climb well above seasonal \naverages. High temperatures will soar into the low to mid 60s \nstarting tomorrow, which will be 15 to 20 degrees above normal \nfor this time of the year! Lows will be above freezing in the \n40s. Friday and Saturday will be the warmest days of the week, \nwith near record high temperatures.\n\nAs the ridge breaks down by late in the week, rain chances return on \nSunday as a cold front moves through. Compared to yesterday, models \nhave slowed down the timing of this front into our area by about 12 \nhours; whereas before it looked like we could get some \nscattered showers Saturday night, now it appears like Saturday \nwill stay dry and Sunday now has the bulk of the rain chances. \nBy the time it makes its way into our forecast area, the cold \nfront should be fairly weak. With a limited amount of \ninstability and potentially unfavorable morning timing to work \nwith, only light rainfall amounts of 0.10-0.25\" are anticipated \non Sunday. Have maintained low to medium PoPs on Sunday with \n20-40% chances. Following the frontal passage, temperatures will\nmoderate back towards seasonable levels with highs in the 40s. \nThere are no real signs of winter yet as the mild weather \npattern looks to hold at least through the end of meteorological\nfall.",
